# Vallo environment template. Copy to .env.local for local dev and mirror
# every value into the deploy platform (Vercel: Project Settings, Environment
# Variables). Never commit .env.local. Keys marked SERVER ONLY must never get
# the NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix - that prefix compiles the value into the browser
# bundle, where anyone can read it.
#
# Nothing here throws on startup. Every integration is checked lazily and
# degrades to an honest state, so the platform boots and is walkable with this
# file completely empty. docs/ENVIRONMENT.md says exactly what each absence
# costs and where to go and get the credential.

# =========================================================== REQUIRED
# Without these four there are no accounts, no listings, and every picker
# reports that it could not load its list.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Supabase
# Dashboard: https://supabase.com/dashboard/project/uccixoonmbhrnyczyigt/settings/api
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://uccixoonmbhrnyczyigt.supabase.co
# The anon / publishable key. Public by design: it only ever acts through Row
# Level Security. If the sign-up dropdowns are empty in production, check this
# one first.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
# SERVER ONLY. Service role bypasses RLS; used by admin jobs only. Reading it
# in a browser bundle throws by design.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- Platform
# The deployment's own public URL. Auth redirects and every link inside an
# email are absolute, so this must match where the app actually runs. On Vercel
# preview builds the platform's own VERCEL_URL is used automatically when this
# is unset; set it explicitly for production.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=

# =========================================================== OPTIONAL
# Each one switches a feature on. Each absence has a designed, honest state.

# ------------------------------------------------- Auth providers (Supabase)
# Which social sign-in buttons to offer, comma separated: google, apple.
# Leave empty to offer email only. Only list a provider AFTER enabling it in
# the Supabase dashboard, because listing one that is not enabled sends people
# to an error page. The provider secrets themselves live in Supabase, never
# here:
#   Supabase Dashboard: Authentication > Providers > Google / Apple
#   Google OAuth client: https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials
#   Apple Sign In:       https://developer.apple.com/account/resources/identifiers
# Apple is not optional once Google is offered: App Store guideline 4.8.
# Optional override. Leave unset for the defaults: Google on, Apple off.
# Name the providers you want ("google,apple"), or "none" for email only.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_AUTH_PROVIDERS=

# ------------------------------------------------------------- AI assistant
# SERVER ONLY. Powers the assistant and the support escalation summariser.
# Absent: the assistant answers with an honest "not configured" message instead
# of pretending, and support falls back to its keyword FAQ store.
# Get a key: https://console.anthropic.com/settings/keys
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# Optional model pins, one per route. Unset, each route uses the default
# model named in its own route file (`app/api/assistant/route.ts`,
# `app/api/support/route.ts`).
ASSISTANT_MODEL=
SUPPORT_MODEL=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Payments
# Paystack (Nigerian cards, transfers, USSD):
# https://dashboard.paystack.com/#/settings/developers
# SERVER ONLY. The server initialises each transaction and hands the page its
# access code; the page resumes it in Paystack's inline iframe
# (`components/app/payments/PaystackCheckout.tsx`), which needs no public key.
# So the browser never holds a Paystack key and there is no public one to set.
PAYSTACK_SECRET_KEY=

# The bearer token that guards /api/paystack/reconcile.
#
# WITHOUT THIS, RECONCILIATION CANNOT RUN AT ALL. The route refuses every
# request with 401 when it is unset, which is the correct default for an
# endpoint that can post money into wallets, and it is also the reason to set
# it before you need it rather than during an incident.
#
# What it is for. Paystack takes a payment, and the webhook that tells us so
# can fail to arrive: a missing key, a bad signature, a deploy mid-delivery, a
# dashboard URL nobody updated. When that happens the charge succeeded and our
# ledger never learned, which has already happened on this platform once and
# is the reason any of this exists. The sweep asks Paystack what it actually
# charged, compares that against wallet_entries and transactions, and reports
# or posts whatever is missing. It is idempotent on the payment reference, so
# it is safe to run repeatedly and safe to run against itself.
#
# Set it to a long random string, for example `openssl rand -hex 32`.
#
# Read it first, apply second:
#   GET /api/paystack/reconcile?hours=72           reports gaps, writes nothing
#   GET /api/paystack/reconcile?hours=72&apply=1   posts them
# Both take `Authorization: Bearer <this value>`, which is the header Vercel
# Cron sends, so the same secret schedules it. SERVER ONLY.
RECONCILE_CRON_SECRET=

# -------------------------------------------------------- Transactional email
# Resend (booking confirmations, receipts): https://resend.com/api-keys
# SERVER ONLY. Absent: sendEmail returns {sent:false, reason:"unconfigured"}
# and nothing leaves the process.
RESEND_API_KEY=
# The From address on every platform email. Must be a verified sender on the
# Resend domain, or delivery is rejected. Defaults to
# "Vallo <hello@vallospaces.com>" when unset (`lib/email/client.ts`).
# SERVER ONLY.
EMAIL_FROM=

# Where a reply to any platform email lands. Unset, no reply_to is sent at all
# and a reply goes to the From address, which nobody reads. Takes either
# "hello@vallospaces.com" or "Vallo <hello@vallospaces.com>". A message that
# sets its own reply address still wins. This is printed in the headers of
# every message the platform sends, so it is as public as the From line and
# must never be a private mailbox. SERVER ONLY.
EMAIL_REPLY_TO=

# ---------------------------------------------- Removed: outside inventory
# LITEAPI_KEY, LITEAPI_WHITELABEL_DOMAIN, LITEAPI_BOOKING_HOST and
# GOOGLE_PLACES_API_KEY used to live here. Nothing reads them any more and
# setting them configures nothing.
#
# Vallo carries first-party listings only. Every property on the platform was
# put there by a person on the platform: a landlord, an owner selling, or an
# agent. That is what makes the verification ladder mean something, what gives
# every listing somebody to message and somebody accountable for what it says,
# and what lets money move through escrow instead of to a stranger.
#
# Hotels, restaurants and shortlets are still categories a person can list.
# What went is the import, never the category.

# --------------------------------------------------------------- Copy gates
# Six surfaces show a support address. Until this names a real mailbox they
# show the in-app route instead of an address that would bounce.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPPORT_EMAIL=
# Naira to US dollar, e.g. 1600. The wallet's currency toggle does not appear
# until this is set. Gated rather than defaulted on purpose: an invented rate
# on a wallet balance is a lie about somebody's money.
NEXT_PUBLIC_NGN_USD_RATE=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Crypto
# SERVER ONLY. Powers the side-nav Crypto surface (prices, movers, coin
# detail) through /api/crypto/*. Absent: the markets and coin routes answer
# {ok:false, reason:"unconfigured"} and the surface draws its dark state; the
# keyless public endpoint is deliberately never called. DEX pairs come from
# GeckoTerminal, which needs no key. Display only: nothing here trades, holds
# or advises. Get a key: https://www.coingecko.com/en/developers/dashboard
COINGECKO_API_KEY=
# `demo` (default) or `pro`. Chooses the CoinGecko host and the header the key
# travels in; a demo key on the pro host is refused, so match it to the plan
# the key was issued under.
COINGECKO_PLAN=

# Yellow Card (crypto top-ups into the naira wallet), `lib/payments/yellowcard.ts`
# and `docs/wallet/CRYPTO_DEPOSITS.md`. SERVER ONLY. Absent: the crypto top-up
# answers "not configured" and nothing is called. The base URL, signing and
# field names are written from Yellow Card's published API and have not yet
# run against a real merchant account. Supplied by: Yellow Card, on merchant
# onboarding.
YELLOWCARD_API_BASE=
YELLOWCARD_API_KEY=
YELLOWCARD_API_SECRET=

# ------------------------------------------------------ Inspection reports
# Feature switch, `lib/inspections/report-flag.ts`. Unset (the default) the
# eight-room inspection report is ON. Set to 0 to turn it off without a code
# change: the rows still draw but cannot be ticked, and nothing claims to have
# been saved.
VALLO_INSPECTION_REPORTS=

# ----------------------------------------------- Local fixture harnesses
# `1` opens the `(dev)/preview` and `/gallery` fixture harnesses on a local
# `next start`. They answer not-found on Vercel whatever this says. Local only.
VALLO_PREVIEW_HARNESS=

# ------------------------------------------------ Public catalogue
# Off unless `1`, `true` or `on`. On, signed-out visitors can read the
# catalogue (search, stays, restaurants and their detail pages); everything
# else stays behind sign-in. Read per request by `proxy.ts`. SERVER ONLY.
# See docs/PRODUCT.md section 4.
VALLO_PUBLIC_CATALOGUE=

# ------------------------------------------------ AI spend ceiling rates
# The kobo cost per million tokens that the Around assistant's monthly and
# daily spend ceilings count with (`lib/social/bot-schema.ts`). Unset, the
# published price of the configured model at 1,600 naira to the dollar. Set
# them when the exchange rate or the model price moves. SERVER ONLY. Never
# shown to anybody.
BOT_INPUT_KOBO_PER_MTOK=
BOT_OUTPUT_KOBO_PER_MTOK=

# ------------------------------------------------------- Build and native
# A separate Next.js output directory, so two local builds cannot corrupt each
# other's `.next` (`next.config.ts`). Local only; the deploy sets nothing.
NEXT_DIST_DIR=
# The live origin the native shell loads (`capacitor.config.ts`). Set in every
# shell and CI job that runs `npx cap sync`; unset, the app opens on the
# offline card. See docs/MOBILE_READINESS.md.
CAPACITOR_SERVER_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Map tiles
# THE ONE ITEM ON THIS LIST THAT IS A LICENCE RATHER THAN A FEATURE.
# Unset, the map draws on CARTO's public basemaps, which are free and
# NON-COMMERCIAL USE ONLY - and a marketplace taking a booking fee is a
# commercial use. Set this before money moves through the deployment.
# Get a key: https://cloud.maptiler.com/account/keys
# The map switches provider, zoom ceiling and attribution together the moment
# it is present; nothing else has to change.
NEXT_PUBLIC_MAPTILER_KEY=

# --------------------------------------------------- Content Security Policy
# The policy itself is always on. This decides only whether a violation is
# BLOCKED or merely REPORTED, and it defaults to reporting.
#
# Leave it unset until the deployment log is quiet. Every violation posts to
# /api/csp-report and is written as one `[csp]` line, and those lines are the
# list of things that would break the day this is switched on. When there are
# none left for a few days across real traffic, set it to the literal `true`.
# Anything else, including a typo or `TRUE`, keeps reporting, which is the safe
# direction for a value that can white-screen the site.
CSP_ENFORCE=

# ------------------------------------------------------------- Data source
# `repository` (default) or `api`. Selects the listing and agent data source.
# Leave unset unless you know why you are changing it. Only the exact string
# `api` switches it; anything else, including a typo, is the default.
NF_DATA_SOURCE=

# --------------------------------------------------- Crash and error reporting
# The Sentry DSN, server side only. Leave it unset locally: with no value the
# reporter does nothing at all and prints nothing at all, so the console stays
# readable.
#
# SET IT BEFORE THE STORES. An App Store or Play Store build that crashes
# gives us nothing back; neither store forwards that data, so the only record
# of the first real user's crash is this. Server errors are caught by
# instrumentation.ts, browser errors by the error boundaries and
# /api/client-error.
#
# There is deliberately NO NEXT_PUBLIC_ twin. The browser posts to our own
# origin and the server forwards, so the DSN never reaches a bundle, the
# Content Security Policy needs nothing added, and there is one scrubber
# rather than two.
#
# Nothing personal ever leaves: every event passes an explicit allowlist and
# then the credential-key vocabulary in lib/alerts/record.ts. See
# docs/ENVIRONMENT.md section 3.
SENTRY_DSN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------- Paging a human
# OPS-03. A CRITICAL alert (a refused catalogue read, the catalogue canary, a
# silent cron, a money job failure) is sent to a person, at most once an hour
# per alert, through lib/ops/page.ts. Either or both. SERVER ONLY. Unset,
# alerts stay rows on /admin/alerts and nobody is told.
#   OPS_ALERT_EMAIL        an address somebody reads on a phone. Sent through
#                          Resend directly (needs RESEND_API_KEY), not the
#                          outbox, whose drain is itself a cron.
#   OPS_ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL  an https URL taking a JSON POST {"text","title"}:
#                          https://ntfy.sh/<a long random topic> is the
#                          simplest (install the ntfy app, subscribe to it).
OPS_ALERT_EMAIL=
OPS_ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- Testing
# Read only by the Playwright specs in apps/web/tests, never by the product.
# Each spec falls back to its own localhost port, so leave this unset unless
# you are pointing the suite at a deployed build.
BASE_URL=

# ============================================ NOT WIRED TO ANYTHING YET
# Listed so nobody wastes an afternoon wondering why setting them changed
# nothing. No code reads any of these today.
#
#   TERMII_API_KEY            Phone/SMS OTP has not shipped.
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY   No analytics client is installed.
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN    Nothing reads it and nothing should: the DSN is
#                             server side only. SENTRY_DSN itself IS wired now
#                             and has its own section above.

# The Send Email Hook's shared secret, from Supabase Dashboard >
# Authentication > Hooks > Send Email. Supabase shows it as
# v1,whsec_<base64>; paste the whole thing. Without it /api/auth/email-hook
# refuses every request, which is deliberate: that endpoint sends mail to any
# address in its body, so an unverified version is an open relay.
SUPABASE_AUTH_HOOK_SECRET=

# The store listings and the social accounts, all optional and all read at
# build time. A store badge falls back to /start (the browser install page)
# until its URL is set, so it is never a link to a listing that is not there.
# A social mark is drawn ONLY when its URL is set, so the footer never shows a
# glyph that opens nothing.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_STORE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_PLAY_STORE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_VALLO_X_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_VALLO_TELEGRAM_URL=

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE STORE REVIEWER ACCOUNT. Read by `npm run seed:reviewer` and by nothing
# else, ever: no application code reads either of these, and neither belongs
# in a deployment's environment. They exist so that the moment the founder
# chooses the login both stores require, seeding it is one command.
#
#   npm run seed:reviewer -- --dry-run   says what it would do, writes nothing
#   npm run seed:reviewer                does it, then PROVES the sign in
#
# The script never prints the password. Absent, it refuses to run and names
# every value it is missing.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
SEED_REVIEWER_EMAIL=
SEED_REVIEWER_PASSWORD=

# ==================================================== PUSH NOTIFICATIONS
# Three transports, three completely different answers to "who supplies this
# and what does it cost". Read that line per variable below rather than
# assuming, because two of the three need the founder and one needs nobody.
#
# NOTHING HERE BREAKS ANYTHING BY BEING ABSENT. `lib/push/credentials.ts` is
# the live authority: `describeCredentials()` prints which transport is short
# of what, by variable name, and the drain refuses to claim queue rows for a
# platform it has no credential for, so a push queued today is still there to
# send the day a key arrives. Nothing is lost by leaving this block empty and
# nothing is faked.
#
# HOW TO SEE IT WORK IN ABOUT A MINUTE: docs/push/FIRST_NOTIFICATION.md.

# ------------------------------------------------- Web Push (VAPID): phones
#                                                    and desktop browsers
# SUPPLIED BY: nobody. You generate this pair yourself, like an SSH key.
# COSTS: nothing. No account, no signup, no company involved.
#
#   npx web-push generate-vapid-keys
#
# THE PAIR IS AN IDENTITY, NOT A PASSWORD. Every subscription a browser makes
# is bound to the public half, so replacing the pair later silently kills
# every device already enrolled and they only come back if the person visits
# again. Generate it ONCE, keep the private half in Vercel and in Vault, and
# do not regenerate it to fix something.
# Public by design: it is compiled into the browser bundle and a browser
# cannot subscribe without it.
NEXT_PUBLIC_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
# SERVER ONLY. The other half of the pair above. This is what signs the
# request to the push service; anyone holding it can send notifications to
# every Vallo device. Never give it the NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix.
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
# Who to contact if our sending misbehaves. A mailto: or an https: URL, and
# it is a real requirement of RFC 8292 rather than a courtesy: some push
# services refuse a request without one. Defaults to hello@vallospaces.com.
VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:hello@vallospaces.com

# --------------------------------- Firebase Cloud Messaging (FCM): the
#                                    ANDROID app only, not the website
# SUPPLIED BY: the founder. It needs a Google account and a Firebase project
# created in a console, which is not something a build session can do.
# COSTS: nothing in money. It costs creating the project.
#
# Console: https://console.firebase.google.com  >  Project settings  >
#          Service accounts  >  Generate new private key
#
# THE SERVER CREDENTIAL ALONE IS NOT ENOUGH, and this is the half people
# forget: the same Firebase project also has to give you
# `apps/web/android/app/google-services.json`, which is a FILE in the repo
# and not a variable. Without that file an Android handset never obtains a
# token, so there is nothing for the key below to send to.
FCM_PROJECT_ID=
# SERVER ONLY. The WHOLE service account JSON, on one line, quoted.
FCM_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON=

# ------------------------------------------- Apple Push Notification service
#                                              (APNs): the iOS app only
# SUPPLIED BY: the founder, and only after buying an Apple Developer
# membership.
# COSTS: 99 USD a year. `ios/App/App/App.entitlements` already records that
# this account does not exist. Until it does there is no iOS push and no
# `aps-environment` entitlement that will build, and no amount of code
# changes that.
#
# Portal: https://developer.apple.com/account/resources/authkeys/list
#         Create a key with Apple Push Notifications service enabled. The .p8
#         downloads ONCE and cannot be downloaded again.
APNS_KEY_ID=
APNS_TEAM_ID=
# SERVER ONLY. The contents of the .p8 file, newlines and all.
APNS_PRIVATE_KEY=
APNS_BUNDLE_ID=com.vallospaces.app
# `true` ONLY for a build signed for the App Store or TestFlight. A
# development build talks to the sandbox, and sending a sandbox token to the
# production host is answered with a 400 that says nothing useful.
APNS_PRODUCTION=false
